</style>
<main class="site-doc" lang="en"><header><div class="wrap"><h1>Tutorials</h1><p>Create a page, design its layout, add a Task Set, and publish with eServer.</p><p><a class="task-jump" href="#task-set-tutorial">Jump to the live Task Set walkthrough</a> ? <a class="task-jump" href="#project-admin-task-set">See the ProjectAdmin database case study</a> ? <a class="task-jump" href="#database-guide">Follow the database walkthrough</a> &middot; <a class="task-jump" href="#build-a-component">Build a reusable component</a></p></div></header><div class="wrap"><section><h2>Create your first page</h2><ol><li>In ePage Studio, open a project and create a page.</li><li>Add a panel, set its text and properties, and save the page.</li><li>Check that the page directory contains <code>page.xml</code> and appears in the project tree.</li></ol><p><a href="./features?v=task-set#main-interface">See the Studio workspace</a></p></section><section><h2>Build a layout that adapts to the viewport</h2>
  <ol><li>Use a vertical layout for the header and content. Put related controls under one parent in the control tree.</li><li>Use horizontal docking to keep the content panel aligned with the available width. Use named layers when multiple views share the same area.</li><li>Preview at a narrow width and check how labels wrap, which areas scroll, and whether buttons are still easy to reach.</li></ol>
  <p><a href="./features?v=task-set#visual-layout">Review docking and layout</a> or <a href="./samples#layers">try the view-switching example</a>.</p>
</section><section><h2>Add a click event</h2><ol><li>Select a button or panel on the canvas.</li><li>Open its <code>onClick</code> event and add a JavaScript handler.</li><li>Publish and click the control in a browser to verify the result.</li></ol><pre>sender.style.backgroundColor = "#46368b";</pre><p><a href="./samples#layers">Try a view-switching example</a></p></section><section><h2>Publish with eServer</h2><ol><li>Register your project directory in the eServer project list.</li><li>Set the domain and default page where appropriate.</li><li>Open your public page URL. Test links and image responses in addition to the HTML.</li><li>For a page with a Task Set, submit a valid request and verify the response. Then test an invalid input and check that the page reports the expected result.</li></ol><p>For example, the page directory <code>features</code> in this project is available at <code>/ePage.dev/features</code>.</p></section>
<section id="task-set-tutorial">
  <h2>Build a Task Set that runs on eServer</h2>
  <p>Use a Task Set when a browser action needs server-side logic. The live form below invokes the named <code>calculateTotal</code> task defined in this page. The multiplication happens inside Script 2 on eServer.</p>
  <form id="task-total-form">
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Try the live calculation</legend>
      <p><label>Quantity <input type="number" name="quantity" min="1" max="99" value="3" required></label></p>
      <p><label>Unit price in cents <input type="number" name="unitPrice" min="1" max="10000" value="250" required></label></p>
      <p><button type="submit">Calculate on eServer</button></p>
      <p id="task-total-result" role="status">Enter two values and submit to see the result.</p>
    </fieldset>
  </form>
  <p><a href="#project-admin-task-set">See how ProjectAdmin uses Task Set with a database</a>.</p>
  <h3>Set it up in Studio</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the page's Task Set editor and add a named task.</li>
    <li>Add the three scripts below. The definition is saved as a <code>taskSetItem</code> under <code>taskSet</code> in <code>page.xml</code>.</li>
    <li>Call <code>calculateTotal()</code> from a browser event. This example calls it from the form's submit handler.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Script 1 ? Prepare in the browser</h3><p>The form accepts a quantity and a unit price in cents. Write the quantity first and the price second.</p><pre><code>const form = document.getElementById(&quot;task-total-form&quot;);
eio.appendInt32(Number(form.elements.quantity.value));
eio.appendInt32(Number(form.elements.unitPrice.value));</code></pre>
<h3>Script 2 ? Validate and calculate on eServer</h3><p>Read the two values in the same order. Check both ranges on the server before multiplying. A zero reply signals rejection because a valid order always has a positive total.</p><pre><code>var quantity = eio.readInt32();
var unitPrice = eio.readInt32();
var valid = quantity &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; quantity &lt;= 99
    &amp;&amp; unitPrice &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; unitPrice &lt;= 10000;
eio.appendInt32(valid ? quantity * unitPrice : 0);</code></pre>
<h3>Script 3 ? Display the response in the browser</h3><p>Read the single integer from the reply and display it in the result area. This is a separate buffer from the two input fields.</p><pre><code>const total = eio.readInt32();
const result = document.getElementById(&quot;task-total-result&quot;);
result.textContent = total &gt; 0
    ? &quot;eServer total: &quot; + total + &quot; cents.&quot;
    : &quot;eServer rejected the quantity or unit price.&quot;;</code></pre>
  <h3>Use the same pattern with records or forms</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Load a table.</strong> Send the filter, query on eServer, then read the returned data set and populate the table.</li>
    <li><strong>Open a record.</strong> Send its ID, check access on eServer, then fill the details controls with the reply.</li>
    <li><strong>Save a form.</strong> Send the edited fields, validate and save on eServer, then display the result or reload the list.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If a reply displays incorrectly, compare each writer and reader in sequence. Check the browser request for transport failures and the eServer logs for execution errors.</p>
</section>

<section id="project-admin-task-set">
  <h2>Task Set in a database application</h2>
  <p>ProjectAdmin is a separate ePage project for managing tasks and teams. Its main page defines 24 named tasks, including loading a list, creating a task, and editing task information. The screenshot shows the actual <code>getTaskList</code> task in ePage Studio.</p>
  <figure class="task-set-capture">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64," width="735" height="413" alt="ProjectAdmin Task Sets editor with getTaskList selected and Handle Response Results open. The other tabs are Send Request to Backend and Backend Processing.">
    <figcaption>The three tabs correspond to the three scripts. The response checks the status, reads a data set on success, and passes it to the completion callback.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h3>Read a page of results with getTaskList</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Send Request to Backend.</strong> The browser sends the current page index, team selection, and view filter with its session information.</li>
    <li><strong>Backend Processing.</strong> The server checks the session, runs its database queries, and writes a status. On success it follows that status with a data set using <code>eio.appendDataSet</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Handle Response Results.</strong> The browser clears the busy state and reads the status first. If the response succeeded, it calls <code>eio.readDataSet</code> and hands the rows to the calling code.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Create and update records</h3>
  <p><code>taskNewV2</code> sends form values to eServer to create a task. <code>modifyTaskInfo</code> sends the selected record and edits to update it. Both use <code>db.executeNoneQuery</code> for database writes and return a status string for the browser to handle. A read task can return a data set after its status, while a write task can return a status alone.</p>
  <p><a href="#database-guide">Set up a practice database and try the same query and write APIs</a>.</p>
  <p>When building a similar page, define the request fields and the success and failure replies before editing the scripts. Keep the corresponding reads and writes in the same order, and check access on eServer before querying or changing records.</p>
</section>

<section id="database-guide">
  <h2>Connect to a database, then query and edit records</h2>
  <p>This walkthrough uses a SQL Server practice table and the same eServer database APIs used by ProjectAdmin. Run the SQL and Task Sets against a database created for practice.</p>

  <h3>1. Register a connection</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>In the ePage project, create a database connection named <code>DemoDb</code> and point its connection string at the practice database. ProjectAdmin stores its named connection under <code>dbConnections</code> in <code>project.xml</code>.</li>
    <li>Give the practice account access to the table and to the operations you plan to test. Keep the connection string in the server-side project configuration and out of browser scripts and published examples.</li>
    <li>Create the table and seed it using your database client. Here <code>Status</code> is <code>0</code> for open and <code>1</code> for complete.</li>
  </ol>
  <pre><code>CREATE TABLE dbo.DemoTasks (
    TaskId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    Title NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    Status INT NOT NULL CHECK (Status IN (0, 1))
);
INSERT INTO dbo.DemoTasks (TaskId, Title, Status)
VALUES (1, N&#x27;Check page layout&#x27;, 0);</code></pre>
  <p>The XML is shown here with a placeholder for the connection string:</p>
  <pre><code>&lt;dbConnections count=&quot;1&quot;&gt;
  &lt;connection name=&quot;DemoDb&quot;&gt;
    &lt;connectionString&gt;YOUR_PRACTICE_CONNECTION_STRING&lt;/connectionString&gt;
  &lt;/connection&gt;
&lt;/dbConnections&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>In Script 2, <code>db.executeQuery("DemoDb", sql)</code> uses the registered alias. If it returns <code>null</code>, check the alias, eServer's access to the database, the account's permissions, and the SQL in that order.</p>

  <h3>2. Read rows and return selected columns</h3>
  <p>Create a Task Set named <code>loadDemoTasks</code>. Call <code>loadDemoTasks()</code> from a browser event. Script 1 can be empty for this fixed query. In Script 2, execute a <code>SELECT</code> and write a status before the data set.</p>
  <pre><code>var rows = db.executeQuery(&quot;DemoDb&quot;,
    &quot;SELECT TaskId, Title, Status FROM dbo.DemoTasks ORDER BY TaskId&quot;);
if (rows == null) {
    eio.appendString8(&quot;F&quot;);
    es.logWarning(&quot;DemoTasks query failed&quot;);
    return;
}
eio.appendString8(&quot;T&quot;);
eio.appendDataSet(rows, &quot;TaskId,Title,Status&quot;, 0, 20);</code></pre>
  <p>In <code>appendDataSet</code>, the last two arguments are the starting row and the number of rows to return. The explicit column list determines what crosses to the browser. For a single-row lookup, ProjectAdmin uses <code>db.read(result)</code> and a typed getter such as <code>db.getString(result, "columnName")</code>.</p>
  <p>In Script 3, read the status first. With a table body named <code>taskTable</code> whose columns match the returned fields and a status panel named <code>taskMessage</code>, the response handler can be:</p>
  <pre><code>var status = eio.readString8();
if (status === &quot;T&quot;) {
    var rows = eio.readDataSet();
    ee.setTableData(ee.getControl(&quot;taskTable&quot;), rows, 0);
} else {
    ee.setText(ee.getControl(&quot;taskMessage&quot;), &quot;Could not load tasks.&quot;);
}</code></pre>

  <h3>3. Update the row selected in the browser</h3>
  <p>Create a named write task such as <code>updateDemoTask</code>. In Script 1, read the selected row ID and the new status from controls. Here the form inputs have the IDs <code>task-id</code> and <code>task-status</code>. Require a positive integer ID and a status of 0 or 1 in the form.</p>
  <pre><code>eio.appendInt32(Number(document.getElementById(&quot;task-id&quot;).value));
eio.appendInt32(Number(document.getElementById(&quot;task-status&quot;).value));</code></pre>
  <p>In Script 2, read both integers in the same order and validate them again. In an authenticated application, also verify that the current user may modify that row before executing the statement.</p>
  <pre><code>var taskId = eio.readInt32();
var status = eio.readInt32();
if (taskId &lt; 1 || (status !== 0 &amp;&amp; status !== 1)) {
    eio.appendString8(&quot;F&quot;);
    return;
}

var sql = &quot;UPDATE dbo.DemoTasks SET Status = &quot; + status
    + &quot; WHERE TaskId = &quot; + taskId;
var ok = db.executeNoneQuery(&quot;DemoDb&quot;, sql);
eio.appendString8(ok ? &quot;T&quot; : &quot;F&quot;);</code></pre>
  <p>Script 3 reads the one status value with <code>eio.readString8()</code>. On success, call <code>loadDemoTasks()</code> to refresh the list. On failure, keep the edit visible and show a message. A successful statement is distinct from an affected row. If the interface must report a missing or concurrently changed record, use a write operation that reports the affected row count or a database routine that returns that outcome explicitly.</p>

  <pre><code>var ok = eio.readString8() === &quot;T&quot;;
ee.setText(ee.getControl(&quot;taskMessage&quot;),
    ok ? &quot;Update submitted.&quot; : &quot;Could not update the task.&quot;);
if (ok) loadDemoTasks();</code></pre>

  <h3>4. Insert and delete practice rows</h3>
  <p>Use <code>db.executeNoneQuery</code> for commands that do not return a result set. The following separate Script 2 examples use fixed demo values. Run the insert before the delete to test both operations.</p>
  <pre><code>var ok = db.executeNoneQuery(&quot;DemoDb&quot;,
    &quot;INSERT INTO dbo.DemoTasks (TaskId, Title, Status) &quot;
    + &quot;VALUES (2, N&#x27;Review task list&#x27;, 0)&quot;);
eio.appendString8(ok ? &quot;T&quot; : &quot;F&quot;);</code></pre>
  <pre><code>var ok = db.executeNoneQuery(&quot;DemoDb&quot;,
    &quot;DELETE FROM dbo.DemoTasks WHERE TaskId = 2&quot;);
eio.appendString8(ok ? &quot;T&quot; : &quot;F&quot;);</code></pre>
  <p>For production forms, use a database access path that binds parameters for user-supplied text, and apply authorization before each write. The update example above constructs SQL only from validated integer fields, and the insert and delete use fixed values. Always scope an update or delete to the intended record.</p>
</section>

<section id="build-a-component">
  <h2>Build a reusable component from a Div</h2>
  <p>In ePage, a Panel can be the root of a component. Its children make up the interface, custom properties configure each instance, and JavaScript in <code>onCreate</code> implements the behavior. The surrounding page calls the component's methods or reacts to its events.</p>
  <h3>See the component in a layered application</h3>
  <p>The Faith desktop main page defines 909 controls and 121 named layers in one <code>page.xml</code>. In Studio, open <strong>pc / main</strong> and select <strong>Explorer</strong> to trace the control tree. Start at the main container and follow the central content group (<code>P2</code>) to views such as Home, Search, and Notifications. The menu, side content, secondary views, and dialog overlays have their own groups. A layer groups controls that appear together; a component such as the editor can appear in several of those groups.</p>
  <h3>1. Start with a Panel and two children</h3>
  <p>Use one child panel for the label and one for the editable area. On the Home instance, the children have local names <code>c_label</code> and <code>c_edit</code>. In <code>onCreate</code>, resolve children within the instance rather than looking up a page-wide ID. The same internal names can then be used in multiple editors on one page.</p>
  <h3>2. Define properties and events in User Property</h3>
  <p>The original editor declares <code>editFontFamily</code>, <code>editFontColor</code>, <code>editFontSize</code>, and <code>editMaxY</code>. Property definitions are stored in <code>userProperty</code> and instance overrides are saved under <code>__U_</code> fields. The following simplified declarations show the format using the actual property and event names.</p>
  <pre><code>//#P:editMaxY|Multiline editor|300px|string||Maximum editor height
//#P:onHeightChange(sender,oldH,newH)|Multiline editor||jsMix||Editor height changed</code></pre>
  <p>The three editor instances in the reference page reuse the same property definitions. The Home composer and mobile composer have a <code>300px</code> limit, while the posting dialog has a <code>150px</code> limit.</p>
  <h3>3. Implement behavior and expose methods</h3>
  <p>This simplified <code>onCreate</code> starts with the same two-child structure as the project. It makes the second child editable and exposes methods on <code>sender</code> so the parent can read or replace its text without reaching into the editable child.</p>
  <pre><code>const editor = sender.eeChildren[1];
editor.contentEditable = &quot;plaintext-only&quot;;
editor.style.whiteSpace = &quot;pre-wrap&quot;;
editor.style.maxHeight = sender.editMaxY;
editor.style.overflowY = &quot;auto&quot;;

sender.getEditText = function () {
    return editor.innerText;
};
sender.setEditText = function (text) {
    editor.textContent = text;
};</code></pre>
  <p>The project editor also tracks text, HTML, and caret position. It measures its content height, updates the outer panel, and calls <code>onHeightChange(sender, oldH, newH)</code> when the size changes. Once it reaches its limit, the editable area scrolls.</p>
  <h3>4. Keep page-specific actions in event handlers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>onInputChange</code> receives the key and before-and-after text and HTML. A handler can return <code>false</code> to cancel an edit.</li>
    <li><code>onFocusChange</code> reports whether the editable area has focus.</li>
    <li><code>onCurPosChange</code> receives the old and new caret positions. Faith uses it to check what the user is typing around an <code>@</code> mention.</li>
    <li><code>onHeightChange</code> lets the parent reflow the surrounding page. On Home, it refreshes the surrounding composer layout.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Reuse the Panel, its child controls, property declarations, and initialization together. Keep draft text and focus state on the editor instance. A page can send the text to a Task Set when the user submits it.</p>
  <p><a href="./samples?v=components#advanced-edit">Try two instances of the advanced editor</a></p>
</section>
</div></main>`;
sender.querySelector("#task-total-form").addEventListener("submit", function (event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    sender.querySelector("#task-total-result").textContent = "Waiting for eServer...";
    calculateTotal();
});

taskSetItem.script2:
var quantity = eio.readInt32();
var unitPrice = eio.readInt32();
var valid = quantity >= 1 && quantity <= 99
    && unitPrice >= 1 && unitPrice <= 10000;
eio.appendInt32(valid ? quantity * unitPrice : 0);

taskSetItem.script3:
const total = eio.readInt32();
const result = document.getElementById("task-total-result");
result.textContent = total > 0
    ? "eServer total: " + total + " cents."
    : "eServer rejected the quantity or unit price.";
